    "title": "Project Research Summary",
    "project": "Cinedise Portal, milestone v3.0 Studio Media Workflow",
    "domain": "Studio media ingest, custody and proxy workflow on a self-hosted, power-fragile laptop-NAS (Rust/axum + SQLite WAL + Next.js 16)",
    "scope": null,
    "researched": "2026-10-24",
    "confidence": {
     "level": "MEDIUM",
     "note": "MEDIUM overall. HIGH on the backend integration seams and the crate/tool choices. MEDIUM on host mount and namespace behaviour (unverified until spikes run). LOW on C50 proxy naming and Resolve relink."
    },
    "exec": [
     "v3.0 adds five capabilities to the shipped v2.0 control plane: projects built from one per-tier template, DIT-style card ingest that leaves two verified copies on two physical devices, lifecycle proposals driven by project completion date, a removable vault that can be checked out for grading and checked back in, and ffmpeg proxies for non-RAW cameras. Commercial offload tools stop at \"copy, verify, report\". The studio-specific value is everything after that: custody of the vault, per-tier project layout, and admin-approved moves between tiers. Almost all of it attaches to the existing job engine. The one genuinely new execution path is an in-process ingest executor that follows the existing archive executor pattern. The decision logic (target planning, lifecycle proposals, C50 pairing) is written as pure functions over snapshots so it can be tested without a real mount.",
     "The recommended approach is deliberately conservative. The backend adds one crate (`xxhash-rust`) and otherwise uses host tools already installed (ffmpeg/ffprobe 8.0.1, `systemctl`, `udisksctl`, `smbcontrol`). The frontend adds no runtime dependencies; it ports labelore's shell and component structure onto the portal, but not its router, query cache or 22k-line stylesheet, and keeps preset `b3Dqcuo4na` strictly. Build order is foundation first (migration 0007, a claim gate that refuses to write into an unmounted tier, a mount-identity guard, and the hash/read-back engine with its byte-flip acceptance test), with the UI shell in parallel. \"Safe to wipe\" is a predicate over durable per-file and per-copy rows, requiring verified copies on two distinct block devices. It is never a boolean a handler sets.",
     "The main risks are all about false confidence. (1) A verify that reads the page cache rather than the disk would let a card be wiped on a copy that never reached the media. (2) An unmount performed inside the service's `ProtectSystem=strict` mount namespace detaches only the portal's view, so the drive looks gone while it is still mounted on the host and in Samba. (3) With the vault checked out, the unmounted mountpoint is an empty directory on a 98 GB root LV with 39 GB free, so writes can land there silently. (4) The desktop automount of a card is not readable by the service user, so card access needs a udev-driven read-only mount. (5) C50 proxy pairing and Resolve relink are unverified until spikes 001b and 002 are closed. Each has a named gate below. Several researchers also disagree on specifics; those conflicts are listed explicitly and need a decision before roadmap creation."
    ],
