     "architecture": {
      "title": "Architecture Approach",
      "line": 65,
      "lede": "The architecture extends the existing engine. Ingest gets a third executor arm (`Tool::Ingest`), modelled on `Tool::Archive`: its own `drive_ingest_job` in a new file, a dedicated OS thread, and cooperative cancel through `RunningHandles`. Per-file and per-copy state lives in child tables written per file, not per chunk. Proxies run as `Tool::Ffmpeg` in a separate lane that takes no tier locks, so a multi-hour render never blocks ingest or lifecycle. Custody is an orthogonal field, not a fourth `TierState`; the enum stays at three variants, pinned by a test. The claim path gains an availability gate, the single most important safety change: a job must never be claimed to write into an unmounted tier."
     },
     "pitfalls": {
      "title": "Critical Pitfalls",
      "line": 82,
      "lede": "Also tracked: clip-name collisions across cards (roll identity in every path; no `--ignore-existing`); card access (udev read-only mount at `/mnt/ingest`, `RESOLVE_BENEATH` containment, no free path picker); proxy truncation and frame or timecode drift (`.partial`, mechanical post-check, Resolve gate); C50 pairing that guesses (ambiguous pairs to a human); CPU and I/O starvation flipping probers to unreachable (lane, thread cap, PSI gate); UI-port regressions (shell first; keep password-change gate and revoked-session redirect)."
     }
